WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.040
SPEAKER 1: Hello, and welcome
to Mental Health Matters,

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.160
Strategies for Managing
Anxiety and Feeling Happier.

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.560
Thanks to all of our
participants who are joining us

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.240
from more than 80
countries around the world.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:14.810
This webinar is brought
to you by Harvard Online.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.990
We are excited to introduce you
to our moderator and panelists

00:00:17.990 --> 00:00:23.840
for this event, Anna Bartuska,
Dr. Luana, and Michael.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:26.360
Without further ado, let's
begin our discussion.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:28.378
Anna, the virtual floor is yours.

00:00:28.378 --> 00:00:29.420
ANNA BARTUSKA: Thank you.

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.300
Welcome, everyone, and we are
delighted to have you with us today.

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:37.280
My name is Anna Bartuska, and
I'm a clinical psychology PhD

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.630
student at the University of Oregon.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.680
It is an honor and a pleasure
to introduce to you today

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.180
two extraordinary experts that
will be guiding our conversations.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.890
First is Michael Puett.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:53.210
Michael Puett is the Walter C.
Klein Professor of Chinese History

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:55.850
and Anthropology at Harvard University.

00:00:55.850 --> 00:00:58.400
His interests are focused
on the interrelations

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.870
between history, anthropology,
religion, and philosophy,

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:05.269
with the hope of bringing
the study of China

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:08.510
into larger historical and
comparative frameworks.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:11.990
He is the author of The
Path, A New Way to Think

00:01:11.990 --> 00:01:15.170
About Everything, The
Ambivalence of Creation,

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.950
Debates Concerning Innovation
and Artifice in Early China,

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:24.630
and To Become God, Cosmology, Sacrifice,
and Self-Divination in Early China.

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:28.580
In addition, he is the course
faculty for Harvard Online's course,

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.690
"The Path To Happiness."

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:33.470
We also have with us today, Dr. Luana.

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:37.160
Dr. Luana is an Associate Professor
at Harvard Medical School,

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.690
Founder and Director of Community
Psychiatry Program for Research

00:01:41.690 --> 00:01:45.200
and Implementation and Dissemination
of Evidence-Based Treatments

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.850
at Massachusetts General Hospital,
and former President of the Anxiety

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:52.700
and Depression Association of America.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.210
Dr. Luana is frequently cited
as one of the leading experts

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.220
in cognitive behavioral therapies.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:02.150
She's been featured in publications,
such as The New York Times, CNN,

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.100
and Harvard Business Review.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.370
She's also been a regular
guest on television broadcasts,

00:02:07.370 --> 00:02:10.970
such as Good Morning America, and
podcasts, including Ten Percent

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:12.140
Happier.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:15.770
Dr. Luana is the author of
Bold Move, A 3-Step Plan

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:18.800
to Transform Anxiety
into Power, which has

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:22.580
been lauded for its clear and practical
approach to dealing with anxiety,

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:25.940
and will be released
officially next Tuesday.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:29.480
Dr. Luana is the course faculty
for Harvard's Online course,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:34.070
"Building Personal Resilience,
Managing Anxiety and Mental Health."

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:38.180
Michael and Luana, we are so excited
to have you both here with us today

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.360
to talk about mental health
and living a good life.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.270
To begin, I would love
to hear from each of you

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:47.090
how you became interested in this topic.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.337
Michael, do you want to kick us off?

00:02:49.337 --> 00:02:50.670
MICHAEL PUETT: I'll be happy to.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.810
Thank you, it's such an honor
to be here with the two of you,

00:02:53.810 --> 00:02:55.520
and with the larger group.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:00.470
I got interested in this topic just
because I was doing two things.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:06.210
I was reading a lot of Chinese
philosophy, and I was living my life.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:12.200
And like for many of us, living
my life with was not always easy.

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:16.580
And meanwhile, I was
reading a body of philosophy

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:21.020
that gave extremely
practical advice for what

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.500
one could do on literally
a daily basis in one's most

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:27.410
sort of mundane aspects of living.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.050
And so my excitement
really started there.

00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:32.810
I decided these texts were
probably onto something,

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.600
and these practices
really would be revealing.

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.187
And that began my path
that has led here.

00:03:39.187 --> 00:03:42.020
ANNA BARTUSKA: I appreciate your
openness to share your own journey,

00:03:42.020 --> 00:03:44.750
and we hope to hear a little
bit more about that today, too.

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:49.392
Dr. Luana, what brought you to
become interested in this topic?

00:03:49.392 --> 00:03:51.350
LUANA MARQUES: Thank you,
Anna, for moderating.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:55.310
It's so nice to be here
with you as well, Michael.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:04:00.800
For me, it's been a transition
really from Brazil to the US.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:05.480
Not until very recently, I realized
that I grew up as a very anxious kid.

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:07.920
And it was really tough growing up.

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:10.760
There was a lot of adversity,
a lot of trauma in my home.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:17.510
And my mom really did the best she could
to get us out of our humble beginnings

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:18.980
and into the US.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.180
And I realized at about
age 15, that I was, well,

00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:26.790
my grandmother, let's be honest, not
I. At 15, I had no insight in this.

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.110
My grandmother really caught
me avoiding a lot of things.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:34.670
And it pushed me to do what's
now known as exposure therapy.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:38.670
And at that point, she really
changed the trajectory of my life.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.930
And I remember coming to the US, first
trying to become a medical doctor,

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:42.920
get an MD.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.803
And I was in Brazil talking to my
grandmother, talking about how much

00:04:45.803 --> 00:04:47.720
I love the brain, and
how much I love science,

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.670
how much I wanted to
sort of help people.

00:04:49.670 --> 00:04:52.250
And she's like, why don't
you take a psychology class?

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:52.970
And so I did.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.932
I took psychology class, and
it just made sense in my brain.

00:04:55.932 --> 00:04:57.890
It's almost like the
things that she had taught

00:04:57.890 --> 00:05:00.920
me now made sense scientifically.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:02.400
And I never looked back.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.548
The rest became history.

00:05:03.548 --> 00:05:05.090
ANNA BARTUSKA: Thank you for sharing.

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:09.440
It seems like mental health has
been a topic for many, many years,

00:05:09.440 --> 00:05:14.000
from ancient Chinese philosophy,
also throughout the duration of both

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.160
of your lives.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:18.590
And yet, we're seeing
such a rise in terms

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.500
of mental health being talked
about in news and on social media

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:22.940
and other platforms.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.710
Can you shed some light as to why we're
seeing that uptick of mental health

00:05:27.710 --> 00:05:29.720
being such a hot topic today?

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:31.970
Dr. Luana, you want to start?

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:33.940
LUANA MARQUES: Sure, I'd love to.

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:37.045
I don't know if it's just
a rise in mental health.

00:05:37.045 --> 00:05:38.920
I think there's a couple
of things happening.

00:05:38.920 --> 00:05:44.600
Since the pandemic, most of us have
had a moment that it was just too much.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:48.580
And so there was no other way,
but to talk about brain health

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:51.470
and anxiety and sadness and loneliness.

00:05:51.470 --> 00:05:56.140
So there is an increase on the
shared experience of things

00:05:56.140 --> 00:05:58.180
that fall under mental health.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:03.670
The CDC is showing 30% to 40% of people
suffering clinical levels of anxiety

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.660
and depression after the pandemic.

00:06:05.660 --> 00:06:07.880
And so first I think there's
increasing awareness.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:10.600
Second, increase of shared humanity.

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:14.230
And then I think our lives are
just changing dramatically.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:18.070
And people are still not
sure how to transition fully.

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:23.630
We expected that our lives could go back
to sort of a new normal, so to speak.

00:06:23.630 --> 00:06:27.370
But I think in many ways, we have
transitioned and need to readjust.

00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:31.117
And that readjustment
hasn't happened as much.

00:06:31.117 --> 00:06:31.950
ANNA BARTUSKA: Yeah.

00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:35.460
You mentioned this word
anxiety, and we hear it a lot.

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:37.905
In your professional
opinion, what is anxiety?

00:06:40.932 --> 00:06:43.640
LUANA MARQUES: So, I'm going to
share an example what anxiety is.

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:46.240
So, this morning, I was
getting ready for this panel,

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.852
and I looked at Michael's course, and I
was trying to learn more about Michael,

00:06:49.852 --> 00:06:51.560
and I didn't a lot
about your work, Mike.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.610
So I looked up, and we have
this new course coming up.

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:55.880
And so I looked at our course.

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:59.690
We had like 8,000 people registered, and
I was like, oh, this is really awesome.

00:06:59.690 --> 00:07:03.020
And I looked at Michael's course, and
he had like 100,000 people registered,

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:04.270
so let's just be clear here.

00:07:04.270 --> 00:07:06.730
And my brain went, oh, my God.

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:08.320
I'm totally messing up.

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:10.010
My course is not going to be successful.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:11.620
I'm such a failure.

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:16.150
And so the first thing that happens,
we talk about anxieties, something

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:20.530
happens and our brain pretty
quickly distorts information.

00:07:20.530 --> 00:07:22.990
And my brain at that
point, I had no capacity

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.820
to look how long Michael's
course was open, none of that.

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:27.580
I just had this
narrative of my brain of,

00:07:27.580 --> 00:07:31.018
I am not good enough, which
started to make my heart pound.

00:07:31.018 --> 00:07:32.560
And I was like, finish getting ready.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.460
And I could sense this
vibration of anxiety,

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.210
and I was like, what
am I doing to myself?

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:40.002
And in fact, later on, I was talking
to Nicole, and I was like, so,

00:07:40.002 --> 00:07:41.710
how long has Michael's course been open?

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:42.640
She's like, a year.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:45.310
I was like, oh, OK, so
maybe I'm not that bad.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.740
But that is sort of anxiety for you.

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:51.820
Like, our brains tend to misinterpret
data when we are anxious,

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.940
and jump to conclusions,
and catastrophize,

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.340
which is just what I did this morning.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:07:59.500
So, Michael, I was not
trying to compare with you,

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:02.500
but my brain certainly wanted to.

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.850
[LAUGHS]

00:08:06.263 --> 00:08:07.180
ANNA BARTUSKA: I know.

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:10.510
I almost dare to say that maybe
happiness is a little bit more

00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:16.420
of a comfortable feeling than
anxiety when we think about it.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.450
Michael, I want to toss it
over to you and to just hear

00:08:19.450 --> 00:08:22.660
your thoughts of why mental health
has become such a hot topic,

00:08:22.660 --> 00:08:25.780
and any thoughts you have about
anxiety or the other side of the coin

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:28.417
that we're all seeking,
happiness, as well.

00:08:28.417 --> 00:08:29.500
MICHAEL PUETT: Absolutely.

00:08:29.500 --> 00:08:32.020
And full agreement with Luana here.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.429
I think that is exactly right.

00:08:34.429 --> 00:08:37.850
We have gone through this period
for, of course, the pandemic,

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:42.070
which was devastating for so many of
us for all of the obvious reasons.

00:08:42.070 --> 00:08:44.830
We have now been in a
world of social media

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:48.820
for 15 years that's having
a tremendous impact.

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:53.080
We're facing, obviously, a very
difficult structural future.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:56.720
Climate change, rising inequality,
lack of social mobility.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:09:00.730
So, a growing sense that the future
for us and the next generations

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:02.720
could be increasingly difficult.

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:08.860
So, all of those are playing
into the current rise of anxiety.

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:13.990
And, of course, the sad part of this,
is that although specific things I just

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:17.680
mentioned are new, much of this is not.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:21.700
People have been wrestling with
difficulties of anxiety for, well,

00:09:21.700 --> 00:09:25.270
thousands of years, which is why I'm
looking at text among other things that

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:29.210
are addressing issues that are
incredibly comparable to those

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:30.710
that we're currently wrestling with.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:35.620
So, I think sadly, going back to Luana's
point, it's telling about the way

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:39.470
that our human brain and
our emotions operate.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.830
We don't respond well to situations.

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:47.680
We invent very complex readings of
issues that are really uncalled for.

00:09:47.680 --> 00:09:52.210
We fall into patterns of responses
that are oftentimes very debilitating.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:54.618
And this is part of being human.

00:09:54.618 --> 00:09:56.410
Now, that's the negative
way of putting it,

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:58.368
let me immediately say,
of course, the flipside

00:09:58.368 --> 00:10:01.930
of that is we are capable
of breaking out of all

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:05.560
that I just mentioned, in terms of our
ways of responding to the world, which

00:10:05.560 --> 00:10:09.910
is why, if these are typically
human bad ways of responding,

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:13.330
we are capable of responding very
effectively, which is exactly,

00:10:13.330 --> 00:10:15.880
of course, what Luana
is showing in her work.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:20.110
And yes, Luana, your course is
going to be incredibly successful,

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:22.252
as will your book.

00:10:22.252 --> 00:10:23.710
LUANA MARQUES: Oh, thank you, Mike.

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:27.580
I love that though, I love, if I
may jump in, I love this piece of,

00:10:27.580 --> 00:10:29.470
it is just the human condition.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:33.610
And our brain is sort of wired
to protect and predict, and often

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:37.118
uses data mechanisms really to
make predictions, like my brain

00:10:37.118 --> 00:10:37.660
this morning.

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:42.320
It's just based on growing up poor
and having this belief I'm not enough.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:45.850
And now I'm an adult, I know
I'm enough, and yet, the brain

00:10:45.850 --> 00:10:46.780
wants to malfunction.

00:10:46.780 --> 00:10:48.572
So I love what you
said, Michael, which is,

00:10:48.572 --> 00:10:50.560
we don't have to believe
that narrative, right?

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.450
There is ways to rewrite our brain.

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:57.827
And that's why I love what
you're talking about, as well.

00:10:57.827 --> 00:10:58.660
ANNA BARTUSKA: Yeah.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:11:01.090
It's really interesting to
hear you both talk about this,

00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:03.980
this idea of pattern and responding.

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:08.410
My question to both of you is, if this
is just part of the human experience,

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:12.430
then when do we just kind
of continue on accepting

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:16.420
that it's part of the human experience
and when do we really start to change?

00:11:16.420 --> 00:11:17.170
Or what do we do?

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:22.670
When does this become problematic,
and how do we know when that happens?

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:27.680
Either of you, whoever wants
to respond first, jump in.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:29.620
LUANA MARQUES: Go for it, Michael.

00:11:29.620 --> 00:11:30.820
MICHAEL PUETT: Sure.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:34.240
I would say it is always problematic.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.150
Part of what's sad about the human
condition, is we often don't see it.

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:42.160
So we see it when it hits a crisis
point, when our patterns just

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:47.200
continue to a point where it becomes a
devastating to those around us in ways

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.660
that we're forced to finally realize.

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:52.960
Or devastating to aspects of our
life in ways that we are again,

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.130
forced to realize.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:12:00.970
But sadly, we are capable of living our
daily life with these patterns and just

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:03.640
kind of continuing day by day.

00:12:03.640 --> 00:12:07.510
In fact, doing horrible things
to ourselves and those around us

00:12:07.510 --> 00:12:08.860
without even seeing it.

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:12.490
And that can continue
for an entire life.

00:12:12.490 --> 00:12:14.770
Now, again, that's the bad news.

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:18.370
The flip side of that
is, we are absolutely

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:20.890
capable of breaking out of this.

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:23.500
We are fully capable of doing so.

00:12:23.500 --> 00:12:27.070
All of us in various moments of our
lives have successfully done so.

00:12:27.070 --> 00:12:30.490
And so part of the goal,
is to develop practices

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.690
in which that becomes part of
our regular way of being, instead

00:12:34.690 --> 00:12:37.990
of constantly falling back into
these patterns that are again,

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:41.710
so devastating, and yet, so
normalized in our way of living

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:43.780
that we tend not to even notice them.

00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:48.520
We just think that's what life is,
or what we personally just are.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:51.332
We're just unhappy creatures.

00:12:51.332 --> 00:12:53.540
LUANA MARQUES: I love this,
Michael, I love this idea

00:12:53.540 --> 00:12:59.000
that we have to sometimes hit this
wall and this crisis to see it.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:00.592
And it doesn't have to be that way.

00:13:00.592 --> 00:13:03.800
I think that's such an important message
for everybody listening to us today,

00:13:03.800 --> 00:13:06.110
that you don't have to
get to the breaking point.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:08.960
That, in fact, you can
catch this earlier.

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:10.400
And the signals are there.

00:13:10.400 --> 00:13:12.480
I think when, I talk
a lot about anxiety,

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:14.840
and I think about anxiety
as a high fever, right?

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.680
It's calling your attention.

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.500
It's trying to tell you something.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.850
But anxiety itself is not the problem.

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:23.960
It's what we do when we are
anxious that's the problem, right?

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:26.750
So for me, the example I
shared earlier today, I

00:13:26.750 --> 00:13:29.870
shared not only to sort of share the
vulnerability of myself, but like I

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:32.110
wouldn't look for data, to
talk to Michael's point.

00:13:32.110 --> 00:13:34.443
I was like, wait a minute,
like can I interpret this way

00:13:34.443 --> 00:13:35.970
or is there another way to do this?

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.460
But I could have done the opposite.

00:13:37.460 --> 00:13:40.170
I could have continued to
avoid, which by the way, then,

00:13:40.170 --> 00:13:42.980
would make me super anxious
to be here with you.

00:13:42.980 --> 00:13:47.420
And I love this point of number
one, we all can identify it,

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:51.620
and that we can catch it earlier
and change it in a daily basis.

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:54.350
I use CBT on myself every day.

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:57.050
And that's the only way to I
think to have a happy life.

00:13:59.397 --> 00:14:01.730
ANNA BARTUSKA: There's so
much good happening right now.

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:03.980
I want to slow down the
conversation a little bit,

00:14:03.980 --> 00:14:09.170
Luana, to what you just mentioned, about
this idea of anxiety being the fever.

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:12.440
Can you tell us a little
bit more about this term

00:14:12.440 --> 00:14:15.650
that you use across your
work, psychological avoidance,

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.880
and how it fits into that analogy?

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:22.450
LUANA MARQUES: So, for the last
20 years of my career, everyone

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:25.540
that had come to work with me, be
it a big organization or individual,

00:14:25.540 --> 00:14:29.230
they want me to get rid of anxiety,
just get rid of my anxiety.

00:14:29.230 --> 00:14:33.050
But anxiety really is not
necessarily the problem.

00:14:33.050 --> 00:14:35.990
In fact, we are biologically
wired to have a fear response.

00:14:35.990 --> 00:14:39.520
So, I cannot magically get
somebody's anxiety completely gone.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:42.440
In fact, it's like losing
your pain receptors.

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:45.460
If you touch a hot stove and
you don't have pain receptors,

00:14:45.460 --> 00:14:47.060
it's a really big problem.

00:14:47.060 --> 00:14:50.500
And so some level of
anxiety is expected.

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:54.430
The problem is what I call psychological
avoidance, which is anything

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:58.400
that we do to try to make
ourselves feel better momentarily,

00:14:58.400 --> 00:15:00.835
but it has a long-term
negative consequence.

00:15:00.835 --> 00:15:03.020
So I'm going to give an example.

00:15:03.020 --> 00:15:07.120
If you are afraid of public speaking,
you might just pass on the promotion,

00:15:07.120 --> 00:15:10.540
because you're so anxious, that
you just can't take that promotion.

00:15:10.540 --> 00:15:15.010
If you want to go on a date, but
you're scared of other people,

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:16.420
you might not go on that date.

00:15:16.420 --> 00:15:20.560
And so every time we avoid,
we are physically actually

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.360
training our brain that
we can't handle something.

00:15:23.360 --> 00:15:25.825
And that is the real infection.

00:15:25.825 --> 00:15:28.450
There were many moments in my
personal life that I'm terrified.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:31.670
I was just speaking at Formula
1, and before I went on stage,

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:35.300
my heart felt like it was just
going to jump out of my chest.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:37.210
But I approached, instead of avoided.

00:15:37.210 --> 00:15:41.575
And so that's why I often say,
it's not anxiety, it's what we do.

00:15:41.575 --> 00:15:45.220
And if what we do is avoid, we are
robbing ourselves of a good life.

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:49.920
ANNA BARTUSKA: That's
incredibly insightful.

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.330
I'm curious, Michael, how that
kind of relates to some of the work

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:57.410
that you've done in some of Chinese
philosophy and where it might diverge

00:15:57.410 --> 00:15:58.760
or similar.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.030
Any thoughts.

00:16:00.030 --> 00:16:01.280
MICHAEL PUETT: Full agreement.

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:03.650
That is beautifully insightful.

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:08.870
And yes, so a lot of these texts
I deal with from early China

00:16:08.870 --> 00:16:10.860
will say exactly the same thing.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:14.120
So, the way they will
put it, is to say, we

00:16:14.120 --> 00:16:17.450
will fall into patterns
of responding to the world

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:20.540
that we come just to
accept as who we are.

00:16:20.540 --> 00:16:23.180
So Luana, going back
to your examples, I'll

00:16:23.180 --> 00:16:28.160
just think, oh, I'm just
inherently bad at not.

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:30.770
That speaking publicly,
it's just not me.

00:16:30.770 --> 00:16:36.140
And we'll even defend it with
terminology like, oh, that's just me

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:39.380
and I should learn to love and
embrace myself for who I am.

00:16:39.380 --> 00:16:42.410
Loving my strengths, but
also loving my weaknesses.

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:45.770
And among my weaknesses are that
just I can't speak publicly.

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:47.310
And that's just me.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:51.890
And so I should just find areas
of life where I can just be me,

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:54.260
and therefore, not
have to speak publicly.

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:58.490
And as you said, what we're doing
there, is just normalizing that.

00:16:58.490 --> 00:17:01.040
We're taking these
patterns we've fallen into

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:04.520
and thinking that those
patterns themselves actually

00:17:04.520 --> 00:17:07.760
are just us, that's just who I am.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:10.310
And I agree with you fully.

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:14.780
Rather, what we should be doing, is a
daily practice where we're breaking out

00:17:14.780 --> 00:17:17.849
of our patterns and,
sticking with your example,

00:17:17.849 --> 00:17:22.520
if I feel I cannot speak publicly,
that's not because of who I am,

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:25.700
that's because I've fallen into a
pattern of just being incredibly

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:29.660
fearful of speaking publicly, for
perfectly understandable reasons.

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:32.870
But the way to do that is to
train oneself to speak publicly.

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.840
And like you, I would generalize this.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:40.370
All of these things we just think
we can't do because of who we are,

00:17:40.370 --> 00:17:43.250
realize these are probably just
patterns we've fallen into.

00:17:43.250 --> 00:17:46.670
And all patterns are
changeable and are provable.

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:51.950
And the key is, it's a daily process
of just working through your patterns,

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:56.910
breaking your patterns, and having a
much better way of living in the world.

00:17:56.910 --> 00:18:01.330
So I think the way you've put
it is absolutely beautiful.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:03.810
LUANA MARQUES: I love this
part, Michael, on this idea

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.970
that we even rationalize our patterns.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:09.840
I just think such a good, we create a
narrative, I call this like, my work,

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:11.370
I call this a pretzel effect.

00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:14.940
That like we pretzel the
information just to fit a pattern.

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:18.340
But it doesn't really, if you say it
out loud, it doesn't make any sense.

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:22.380
In fact, if we're talking
to, if we talk to our friends

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:24.960
the way we talk to ourselves,
we would have no friends.

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:27.000
Because we are so mean!

00:18:27.000 --> 00:18:29.680
Just to hold on to something
that makes no sense.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:30.720
I love that piece.

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:34.190
ANNA BARTUSKA: Yeah.

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:37.160
I think that I completely agree.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:41.060
We're going to open it up for
some audience questions here soon.

00:18:41.060 --> 00:18:43.220
And we received some
questions in advance.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:46.820
And I have one that I think really
fits into this conversation a bit.

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:51.080
So we've been talking a lot about this
idea of our patterns get us stuck.

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:52.580
They're part of what makes us human.

00:18:52.580 --> 00:18:55.910
They aren't who we are, but our
patterns, particularly when we

00:18:55.910 --> 00:19:02.090
avoid things, is some of what's leading
to lack of happiness or mental health.

00:19:02.090 --> 00:19:05.240
A participant from Kenya
has asked, "How can I

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:09.716
differentiate between mental
health and normal life stress?"

00:19:09.716 --> 00:19:12.107
Luana, you want you
want to start this one?

00:19:12.107 --> 00:19:12.940
LUANA MARQUES: Sure.

00:19:12.940 --> 00:19:14.990
I love that we have a
participant from Kenya.

00:19:14.990 --> 00:19:17.390
So, I don't know who you
are, but that is awesome.

00:19:17.390 --> 00:19:20.170
I'm so excited that you're
thinking about this.

00:19:20.170 --> 00:19:22.900
And so, how do we
separate day to day stress

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:24.680
versus like getting to mental health?

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:27.385
And I imagine that
behind this question, is

00:19:27.385 --> 00:19:31.480
this idea of am I just stressed, and
it's normal, and I can handle it,

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:34.360
versus like, when do I
also need to seek some help

00:19:34.360 --> 00:19:36.580
and think about what it looks like.

00:19:36.580 --> 00:19:38.860
And the way I always think
about this, and actually,

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:41.110
Michael said this a
little bit already, which

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:44.980
is, is this starting to
affect your day to day life.

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:47.590
Is it taking too much
energy to do things?

00:19:47.590 --> 00:19:50.500
So often, usually when we're
talking about mental health

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:52.900
in the spectrum of
diagnosis, we're talking

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.750
about somebody that is
having trouble sleeping,

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:59.020
they're having trouble concentrating,
they are having trouble

00:19:59.020 --> 00:20:01.460
engaging with the meaningful things.

00:20:01.460 --> 00:20:03.910
And so the way I put
this very simply is,

00:20:03.910 --> 00:20:06.250
what is the price tag
for you stress right now,

00:20:06.250 --> 00:20:08.170
and who is paying that price tag?

00:20:08.170 --> 00:20:10.870
Because often, we are
paying the price tag.

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:12.650
And I love that Michael
said this already.

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:14.480
We don't have to be stuck.

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:17.200
And so if you're sitting
around and you're wondering,

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:19.870
my suggestion is, it's
already had a price tag.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:22.720
And so Michael's point
earlier, let's not

00:20:22.720 --> 00:20:26.230
wait until it becomes a crisis,
which is usually most of us, ugh,

00:20:26.230 --> 00:20:31.270
myself included, and that we
pay much bigger price tag.

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:34.540
Mike, I'd love to hear your
thoughts, too, on this.

00:20:34.540 --> 00:20:36.040
MICHAEL PUETT: I agree completely.

00:20:36.040 --> 00:20:40.250
This is both a wonderful question
and Luana, a wonderful answer.

00:20:40.250 --> 00:20:43.330
So in full agreement, yes, I agree.

00:20:43.330 --> 00:20:46.780
Often, the point where we
think our difficulties have

00:20:46.780 --> 00:20:50.200
moved beyond normal stress
and reached a serious point,

00:20:50.200 --> 00:20:53.110
is that point where indeed,
the patterns have played out

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:56.650
to an area where we can
no longer ignore them,

00:20:56.650 --> 00:21:01.000
where we become clearly undeniably
harmful to ourselves or those

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:01.840
around us.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:07.060
And as you said, Luana, the key is,
we want to try as much as possible.

00:21:07.060 --> 00:21:10.660
We'll fail, but try as much
as possible to not get there.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:15.760
Because even what we accept
as normal life, most of that

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:20.140
is going to be based upon these patterns
that we are dangerously falling into.

00:21:20.140 --> 00:21:23.450
And if we're falling into
them on a daily basis,

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:28.060
and I think we are in general,
that's where on a daily basis,

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:30.170
we need to start working out of them.

00:21:30.170 --> 00:21:33.370
And so going back to
Luana's point, we don't

00:21:33.370 --> 00:21:36.790
want to wait until
something horrible happens.

00:21:36.790 --> 00:21:41.230
The key is, we want to start
these daily practices right away

00:21:41.230 --> 00:21:44.500
and begin working on our
patterns with hopefully,

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:50.530
realization that if we do so, we may
not as often reach those crisis points.

00:21:50.530 --> 00:21:54.370
And also, as we go, I think we'll
realize what we often consider

00:21:54.370 --> 00:21:57.820
to be normal stress isn't just normal.

00:21:57.820 --> 00:22:02.530
It's just because we've rationalized
this as being the normal unhappiness

00:22:02.530 --> 00:22:03.430
that we live with.

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:06.220
And maybe we shouldn't
be telling ourselves

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:08.740
we should be living with that
normal level of unhappiness.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:13.230
So, full agreement, Luana, I
think that's a beautiful answer.

00:22:13.230 --> 00:22:14.340
ANNA BARTUSKA: Yeah.

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:15.540
I really appreciate this.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:18.480
We have a few more questions coming in,
which I'm so glad that they were asked,

00:22:18.480 --> 00:22:21.147
because this is actually something
that I've been thinking, too.

00:22:21.147 --> 00:22:25.590
So you mentioned that in a moment of
crisis, that this is really apparent.

00:22:25.590 --> 00:22:28.500
Like if we're holding hot coffee
and it spills, it's clearly hot.

00:22:28.500 --> 00:22:30.300
But sometimes when
we're holding the cup,

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:34.290
we don't actually realize what inside of
it is hot or is keeping our hands full

00:22:34.290 --> 00:22:37.440
and actually creating a lot
of energy and drain from us.

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.970
So, we have kind of two
questions from the audience,

00:22:39.970 --> 00:22:42.178
and I'm hoping that we can
kind of put them together.

00:22:42.178 --> 00:22:45.600
Someone writes, "Is it possible
that we avoid frequently

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:48.210
for such a long period
of time, that we are

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:53.370
no longer even sure that we're avoiding,
and has become our way of being?"

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:56.670
And similarly related,
someone asks, "How do you

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:59.590
separate a pattern and who you are?

00:22:59.590 --> 00:23:00.790
Where is the difference?"

00:23:00.790 --> 00:23:03.390
And so, how do you identify
this before the moment of crisis

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:06.473
and you have coffee, hot coffee spilled
all over and you realize it's hot?

00:23:08.760 --> 00:23:12.555
Either of you, whoever wants
to start, go ahead and jump in.

00:23:12.555 --> 00:23:13.680
SPEAKER 1: Luana, go ahead.

00:23:13.680 --> 00:23:16.680
LUANA MARQUES: I would jump
in on the avoidance one.

00:23:16.680 --> 00:23:21.660
I love that question about avoidance,
because once the pattern is formed,

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:25.450
our brain, the way our brain is
wired, is to maintain the pattern.

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:28.470
And the reason being, is
it takes a lot less energy

00:23:28.470 --> 00:23:32.910
to believe a distorted pattern than
to actually update your software.

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:35.760
Because when, Mike and I have
been talking about this idea

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:38.370
that we can change, and then we
have to start daily practice.

00:23:38.370 --> 00:23:40.300
But it takes effort to do that.

00:23:40.300 --> 00:23:43.860
And so once you've developed
an avoidance practice,

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:45.390
you tend to keep avoiding.

00:23:45.390 --> 00:23:47.040
Let me give you an example.

00:23:47.040 --> 00:23:49.080
For the longest time,
I thought I was just

00:23:49.080 --> 00:23:51.330
very good at responding to emails fast.

00:23:51.330 --> 00:23:53.580
The emails would come
and I just respond.

00:23:53.580 --> 00:23:56.520
About five years ago, I
realized that what I was doing,

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:57.790
was avoiding my emotions.

00:23:57.790 --> 00:24:02.040
An email'd come, upset me, instead of
being able to tolerate my discomfort,

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:03.677
I just would email really fast.

00:24:03.677 --> 00:24:05.260
And then I would do what Michael said.

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:09.270
I would rationalize, well, I'm
just very fast and I'm very good.

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:11.640
But I was actually paying a price tag.

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:13.860
That's why avoidance is the problem.

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:16.350
Because eventually, somebody
called me in their office

00:24:16.350 --> 00:24:20.280
and they're like, Luana, you don't
have to respond at midnight to an email

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:22.710
that you're clearly upset about.

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:26.580
Because what happens when I'm
responding, I'm just avoiding emotions.

00:24:26.580 --> 00:24:30.090
And because I'm anxious, I'm
not in my thinking brain.

00:24:30.090 --> 00:24:34.770
I'm on my emotional brain, which
means I'm not being logical correct.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:38.910
And so the first piece is, if you are
asking the question, am I avoiding,

00:24:38.910 --> 00:24:40.710
ask yourself one thing.

00:24:40.710 --> 00:24:45.550
If I chose to go against that anxiety,
it's what I call opposite action.

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:46.440
So you're anxious.

00:24:46.440 --> 00:24:49.740
And for me, imagine that I
want to answer that email.

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:52.110
If I don't answer, how would I feel?

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:54.840
And you should feel
slightly more anxious.

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:57.600
Because if you choose to
go opposite to the anxiety,

00:24:57.600 --> 00:24:59.340
you're going to have discomfort.

00:24:59.340 --> 00:25:01.560
But that kind of discomfort
is the discomfort

00:25:01.560 --> 00:25:03.910
we're talking about,
sitting with your emotions,

00:25:03.910 --> 00:25:06.930
so that you can actually
bring their temperature down.

00:25:06.930 --> 00:25:08.910
And so that's how I personally separate.

00:25:08.910 --> 00:25:13.140
I ask myself, you know, in my
dating days, which are long gone,

00:25:13.140 --> 00:25:14.880
but I remember hating dating.

00:25:14.880 --> 00:25:18.390
And I remember like my brain
saying, don't go on that date.

00:25:18.390 --> 00:25:20.550
And if I were to ask
myself, if I go, am I going

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:22.260
to feel more or less uncomfortable?

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:25.780
And at first, I'd feel more
uncomfortable, but why would I do that?

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:27.490
Because I wanted to have a family.

00:25:27.490 --> 00:25:29.020
I wanted to have a partner.

00:25:29.020 --> 00:25:35.518
And so I then chose opposite action from
the avoidance to live a better life.

00:25:35.518 --> 00:25:37.060
I think I took one of your questions.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:38.170
I don't know if I took both, Anna.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.012
I think I got too
excited about avoidance,

00:25:40.012 --> 00:25:41.470
so I'm going to give it to Michael.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:43.420
Maybe he can take the second question.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:45.170
MICHAEL PUETT: I'll
be more than happy to.

00:25:45.170 --> 00:25:46.870
So, wonderful answer.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:49.600
And, yeah, I will then focus a
little bit more in the second,

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:52.370
although it's directly
related to the first as well.

00:25:52.370 --> 00:25:57.850
So, let me say a few words about one
of the ideas that arises in early China

00:25:57.850 --> 00:26:00.200
that I find really telling about this.

00:26:00.200 --> 00:26:01.900
So, first, the contrast.

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:05.260
So certainly in America, but
this has now gone global,

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:07.780
we'll often use terminology
like the following.

00:26:07.780 --> 00:26:13.000
We'll say, oh, I should spend my
life looking within finding myself,

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:14.620
finding my true self.

00:26:14.620 --> 00:26:17.560
And part of that is with the
idea that we mentioned before,

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:20.860
learning to love and embrace
myself for who I really am.

00:26:20.860 --> 00:26:22.520
Now, this sounds great.

00:26:22.520 --> 00:26:25.060
It sounds like the way we
can liberate ourselves.

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:27.910
And going back to the second
part of the second question,

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:31.150
the focus would be what
you're finding there clearly

00:26:31.150 --> 00:26:34.880
is your true self as opposed to these
patterns that we're falling into.

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:39.370
So, let me give a contrasting way
of pointing it from early China.

00:26:39.370 --> 00:26:44.590
A very standard idea that will arise
in early China is, we are messes.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:47.980
We are a mess of stuff when we are born.

00:26:47.980 --> 00:26:53.350
We are a mess of emotions,
desires, faculties, energies,

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.000
and we're all equally messes.

00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:01.060
And then what we become over time,
depends on what we do in life.

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:07.180
Patterns we fall into, practices we
fall into, conventions we are born into,

00:27:07.180 --> 00:27:10.480
which means, going back to the
heart of the second question,

00:27:10.480 --> 00:27:16.000
much of what we often think is
being our true self, the real me,

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:19.905
think of as very likely a bunch of
patterns that you've fallen into.

00:27:19.905 --> 00:27:22.780
Now, that of course, gets to the
heart of that second question, which

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:27.290
is OK, well, if that's true,
is there no self there?

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.470
And the answer is, there definitely is.

00:27:29.470 --> 00:27:31.670
But the self is what you make of it.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:37.750
Which means, think of your life
as you creating and constructing

00:27:37.750 --> 00:27:40.660
an extraordinary self, the way
you would construct a work of art,

00:27:40.660 --> 00:27:43.900
but not in the final moment
when the art is done.

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:49.370
But it's a lifelong process where you
are becoming something extraordinary,

00:27:49.370 --> 00:27:51.730
something beyond what you
could currently imagine,

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:55.480
which means again, going back to
that second question, what you're

00:27:55.480 --> 00:27:59.590
doing on a daily basis is not
finding that true self that's

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:03.660
perfectly acceptable if you're
miserable, but it's you.

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:06.670
You're assuming most of the
patterns you're falling into

00:28:06.670 --> 00:28:09.700
can be worked on and tweaked
and changed and altered.

00:28:09.700 --> 00:28:15.670
And what you will become out of
that process, that's the self.

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:18.520
The self is what you
create through this work.

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:22.870
And it means, getting back to the
heart of Luana's arguments here,

00:28:22.870 --> 00:28:25.540
what you're doing is
breaking out of your patterns

00:28:25.540 --> 00:28:28.660
and learning to really
work with the world,

00:28:28.660 --> 00:28:33.130
build extraordinary relationships in
ways that your patterns will never

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:34.280
allow you to do.

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:38.120
And as long as we keep thinking
those patterns are just who we are,

00:28:38.120 --> 00:28:42.330
we will never be able to break out of.

00:28:42.330 --> 00:28:45.000
LUANA MARQUES: I love
that picture of your life

00:28:45.000 --> 00:28:47.550
is this painting that's
evolving over time.

00:28:47.550 --> 00:28:51.150
And is like, in every day, when
you paint a little corner of it,

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:53.730
it sheds a little more light.

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:57.072
Just such a great image, thank you.

00:28:57.072 --> 00:28:58.530
ANNA BARTUSKA: It is a great image.

00:28:58.530 --> 00:29:00.905
It reminds me of when I was
a kid, you know, and you only

00:29:00.905 --> 00:29:04.860
have the small 12-pack of crayons,
and then over time, you get more,

00:29:04.860 --> 00:29:09.120
and that's like a really
exciting experience.

00:29:09.120 --> 00:29:11.610
I really also appreciate
about your response, Michael,

00:29:11.610 --> 00:29:16.320
that you started to talk about
the hopeful side of this.

00:29:16.320 --> 00:29:19.230
The opportunity to
change for the better.

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:21.420
And it really aligns
with one of the questions

00:29:21.420 --> 00:29:25.680
that we just received from the audience,
that's talking about mental peace.

00:29:25.680 --> 00:29:27.790
And so, that more positive light.

00:29:27.790 --> 00:29:32.550
So the question is, "Is mental peace
based on respecting others patterns,

00:29:32.550 --> 00:29:36.630
or should we push past them
to scramble that pattern?"

00:29:36.630 --> 00:29:41.010
And in parentheses, "just thinking about
family members or partners or friends."

00:29:41.010 --> 00:29:43.530
Michael, what are your initial
thoughts to this question?

00:29:43.530 --> 00:29:45.630
MICHAEL PUETT: Yes, what
a wonderful question.

00:29:45.630 --> 00:29:48.930
And the way I'd like
to address it is, let

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:52.770
me give a couple of analogies, which
might sound like a very weird answer,

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:54.790
but bear with me for a few sentences.

00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:59.070
So, are we capable of
doing the kinds of work

00:29:59.070 --> 00:30:01.270
that Luana and I are talking about?

00:30:01.270 --> 00:30:03.420
And the answer is, absolutely.

00:30:03.420 --> 00:30:06.900
Just think of arenas in our lives
where we do things like this.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:10.810
If you've ever learned a musical
instrument, it's a lot of work.

00:30:10.810 --> 00:30:15.310
You spend years and years
and years training yourself.

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:19.780
But if you succeed after those
years by really doing it,

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:22.920
you can become an extraordinary
musician, either professional, or even

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:27.780
just an amateur, who can feel the
music, feel the fellow players,

00:30:27.780 --> 00:30:29.130
feel the audience.

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:30.870
We're capable of it.

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:32.460
Sports, same thing.

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:37.890
None of us are born able to bounce
a basketball or kick a soccer ball.

00:30:37.890 --> 00:30:41.730
We spend years doing
this and we can train

00:30:41.730 --> 00:30:44.220
ourselves to become extraordinary.

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:48.810
If we are capable of doing this, then
the question would be, why in the world

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:51.390
don't we do this for our daily life?

00:30:51.390 --> 00:30:54.780
We know we are capable
of training ourselves

00:30:54.780 --> 00:30:58.230
to sense the world better,
relate to people better, connect

00:30:58.230 --> 00:30:59.370
with people better.

00:30:59.370 --> 00:31:00.870
We just don't do it.

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:03.540
Now again, that may seem like a
bizarre answer to the question,

00:31:03.540 --> 00:31:05.980
but now let me return
to the heart of it.

00:31:05.980 --> 00:31:09.120
What this would mean is,
if you're in a family,

00:31:09.120 --> 00:31:13.440
do you simply scramble your patterns and
try to become a radically new person?

00:31:13.440 --> 00:31:16.500
No, that would be kind of
like sitting down at the piano

00:31:16.500 --> 00:31:19.230
before you've done your
lessons and playing randomly.

00:31:19.230 --> 00:31:22.950
It would not be fun for you,
and very painful for the people

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:23.970
who had to listen.

00:31:23.970 --> 00:31:27.150
What it would mean, on
the contrary is, you're

00:31:27.150 --> 00:31:29.430
learning to really relate
to your family members.

00:31:29.430 --> 00:31:32.640
You're learning to sense
them in their complexity.

00:31:32.640 --> 00:31:36.240
You're learning not to simply respond
to them by rote pattern, which

00:31:36.240 --> 00:31:38.220
is sadly, the way we often do.

00:31:38.220 --> 00:31:42.040
You're training yourself to
really learn to listen to them,

00:31:42.040 --> 00:31:44.920
and therefore, grow through
those relationships.

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:48.390
You're doing the same thing with
your friends, with the larger world.

00:31:48.390 --> 00:31:52.980
And it's literally the same sort of
work we do if we're learning a sport

00:31:52.980 --> 00:31:54.810
or learning a musical instrument.

00:31:54.810 --> 00:31:58.380
It's about learning to, in
this case, listen to them,

00:31:58.380 --> 00:32:03.570
sense them, grow from them, work with
them, grow through those relationships.

00:32:03.570 --> 00:32:08.880
And what will happen with
your family if you do this?

00:32:08.880 --> 00:32:11.250
It won't be really
scrambling up the patterns.

00:32:11.250 --> 00:32:13.660
It will mean you'll
really relate to them.

00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:17.160
And you will really build
extraordinary relationships,

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:22.230
which mean you and they will grow in
ways you could not now even imagine.

00:32:22.230 --> 00:32:23.980
And it's daily work.

00:32:23.980 --> 00:32:24.900
It's a lot of work.

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:28.773
But like when you're learning a
musical instrument, it's at first,

00:32:28.773 --> 00:32:29.940
it seems like a lot of work.

00:32:29.940 --> 00:32:33.420
But then it comes and
becomes incredibly exciting.

00:32:33.420 --> 00:32:36.270
And I would say it's all the
more so with relationships.

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:40.560
As the relationships become
deeper and you're more connected,

00:32:40.560 --> 00:32:44.880
it just, it's like a snowball effect,
where it just builds on itself

00:32:44.880 --> 00:32:47.430
and you just want to keep
building that relationship.

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:53.220
And going back to the metaphor
of the work of art, again,

00:32:53.220 --> 00:32:56.580
you're forming a work
of art in a way that

00:32:56.580 --> 00:32:58.920
goes beyond what you could
have imagined it would be,

00:32:58.920 --> 00:33:03.340
but it's the process
that is so extraordinary.

00:33:03.340 --> 00:33:04.202
ANNA BARTUSKA: Yeah.

00:33:04.202 --> 00:33:05.410
Thank you for answering that.

00:33:05.410 --> 00:33:08.950
We've had, actually, a lot of
questions come in about really

00:33:08.950 --> 00:33:10.790
how to support others.

00:33:10.790 --> 00:33:14.740
Dr. Luana, I wonder if you could add
a little bit more to this kind of,

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:19.690
if we're playing for learning to play
soccer, what is the first step of what

00:33:19.690 --> 00:33:24.160
we should do to really support others
who are struggling with anxiety

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:28.407
or just feeling unhappy
in their everyday life?

00:33:28.407 --> 00:33:29.240
LUANA MARQUES: Sure.

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:34.070
I love what you said, Michael, because
really imagine a world for a second

00:33:34.070 --> 00:33:37.100
that people would spend as much
time training their brain as they

00:33:37.100 --> 00:33:39.060
do their physical body.

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:43.380
We have guidelines for how much exercise
we should get, and for a good health,

00:33:43.380 --> 00:33:46.610
we have to exercise this many
days a week for those numbers.

00:33:46.610 --> 00:33:49.290
But nobody talks about
training our brain.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:51.800
And so this idea, I think
that's what I heard you say,

00:33:51.800 --> 00:33:55.020
Michael, you need to train your
brain, and it is a lot of work.

00:33:55.020 --> 00:33:56.198
Let's not lie about it.

00:33:56.198 --> 00:33:56.990
It's a lot of work.

00:33:56.990 --> 00:33:59.015
But anything meaningful
is a lot of work.

00:33:59.015 --> 00:34:00.890
You don't get a degree
without a lot of work,

00:34:00.890 --> 00:34:03.960
you don't learn how to play the
piano without a lot of work.

00:34:03.960 --> 00:34:09.179
And so to just go to your
question, Anna, supporting others,

00:34:09.179 --> 00:34:11.570
the first thing we need
to do is first make sure

00:34:11.570 --> 00:34:13.400
that we are supporting ourselves.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:16.880
Because often, I hear that question
about family members and others,

00:34:16.880 --> 00:34:20.030
and you support them, but you,
yourself, are not living your best life.

00:34:20.030 --> 00:34:22.199
Your self is not training your brain.

00:34:22.199 --> 00:34:24.699
And so what happens is, what
Michael has been talking about,

00:34:24.699 --> 00:34:28.139
you just spin with each other instead
of actually being able to do something.

00:34:28.139 --> 00:34:31.580
So this is my secret power
when things are spinning.

00:34:31.580 --> 00:34:32.630
Pause.

00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:33.350
I mean it.

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:35.239
Just pause.

00:34:35.239 --> 00:34:39.170
Because if your family member is
spinning, and often what we do is,

00:34:39.170 --> 00:34:40.820
we try to just go fix it.

00:34:40.820 --> 00:34:43.520
People need to be able to
have their own emotions.

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:46.380
People need to be able to learn
to manage their own emotions.

00:34:46.380 --> 00:34:49.219
So one, is just pause and
observe what's happening.

00:34:49.219 --> 00:34:51.230
Check your own narrative in your brain.

00:34:51.230 --> 00:34:53.750
What are you saying to yourself
about what's happening?

00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:55.190
And the other one is this.

00:34:55.190 --> 00:34:57.170
If you want to really
support a family member,

00:34:57.170 --> 00:34:59.690
ask them what they
need in terms of help.

00:34:59.690 --> 00:35:02.750
We're very creative about
what we think people need.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:05.370
And we often don't ask what they need.

00:35:05.370 --> 00:35:08.130
And sometimes, it's like, I
said to my husband yesterday.

00:35:08.130 --> 00:35:10.005
He's like, what do you
want for Mother's Day?

00:35:10.005 --> 00:35:13.310
I'm like, a nap in the sun in
the hammock, that's all I want.

00:35:13.310 --> 00:35:15.440
He could have done a
million things for me.

00:35:15.440 --> 00:35:17.000
That was the only thing I wanted.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:20.580
And because he asked, he was able
to go play soccer with our son,

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:23.160
and I was able to take my
nap, and it was just perfect.

00:35:23.160 --> 00:35:29.336
And so pause and ask are my two
recommendations for family members.

00:35:29.336 --> 00:35:32.380
ANNA BARTUSKA: Dr. Luana, do you
have any tricks for how to pause?

00:35:32.380 --> 00:35:36.308
I can imagine that would be so hard
if you really want to do something

00:35:36.308 --> 00:35:37.600
or really want to help someone.

00:35:37.600 --> 00:35:40.213
How do we actually pause?

00:35:40.213 --> 00:35:42.630
LUANA MARQUES: Avoidance wants
you to do something always.

00:35:42.630 --> 00:35:43.963
It's always ordering you around.

00:35:43.963 --> 00:35:46.020
So, here's a trick.

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:49.182
When we are anxious or stressed,
we are in our limbic system,

00:35:49.182 --> 00:35:50.640
we are in fight, flight, or freeze.

00:35:50.640 --> 00:35:55.230
We're in anxious brain, which is going
to command you to try to do something.

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:59.617
What we know from cognitive behavioral
therapy, is even the act of writing,

00:35:59.617 --> 00:36:01.200
so imagine that you're really anxious.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:05.820
Putting down your thoughts, linking
thoughts to emotions and to behaviors,

00:36:05.820 --> 00:36:09.480
just by writing simply,
you're actually activating

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:12.120
your prefrontal cortex,
the part of your brain

00:36:12.120 --> 00:36:15.540
that allows you to slow
down, to execute, to plan.

00:36:15.540 --> 00:36:18.838
And so sometimes I am so anxious, that
I can't even think of why I'm anxious.

00:36:18.838 --> 00:36:20.130
I'm like, my brain is spinning.

00:36:20.130 --> 00:36:22.713
I literally take my journal and
I just write down the thoughts

00:36:22.713 --> 00:36:24.780
until I get to oh, this is the thought.

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:26.320
Usually for me is, I'm not enough.

00:36:26.320 --> 00:36:28.320
And then I go oh,
that's that old belief.

00:36:28.320 --> 00:36:29.790
I don't have to do this anymore.

00:36:29.790 --> 00:36:32.760
So just writing, it's
like an awesome trick.

00:36:32.760 --> 00:36:35.600
The other one is going to
sound so odd to many people,

00:36:35.600 --> 00:36:38.100
but I'm going to tell you a
secret from dialectical behavior

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:40.230
therapy that's super
effective, especially

00:36:40.230 --> 00:36:43.800
if you have a toddler at home, like
I do, who at times is melting down.

00:36:43.800 --> 00:36:45.960
And in dialectical
behavior therapy, when

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:49.290
you really, really, really stress,
one technique they use is you

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:52.420
hold two ice cubes, called
the stress tolerance.

00:36:52.420 --> 00:36:55.620
So you just hold an ice cube in your
hand until it burns a little bit.

00:36:55.620 --> 00:36:59.160
That physical pain slows
down the emotional brain,

00:36:59.160 --> 00:37:02.050
and you have a little
window before you act there.

00:37:02.050 --> 00:37:04.850
So that's a little secret.

00:37:04.850 --> 00:37:06.100
ANNA BARTUSKA: That's amazing.

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:08.320
I'm going to ask one more
quick follow-up question.

00:37:08.320 --> 00:37:11.890
You've mentioned this term, emotional
brain and thinking brain, a lot.

00:37:11.890 --> 00:37:16.280
Can you tell us a little bit more about
the science behind those two terms

00:37:16.280 --> 00:37:18.257
and what you mean when you say that?

00:37:18.257 --> 00:37:20.340
LUANA MARQUES: So the way
I think about the brain,

00:37:20.340 --> 00:37:22.048
and it's much more
complicated than this,

00:37:22.048 --> 00:37:24.090
but I try to think of
things very simplistically.

00:37:24.090 --> 00:37:26.070
There's two parts of our
brain that are always

00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:28.230
competing for energy and attention.

00:37:28.230 --> 00:37:30.490
Our thinking brain,
the prefrontal cortex,

00:37:30.490 --> 00:37:33.600
which helps us to do a
math problem, execute plan.

00:37:33.600 --> 00:37:38.440
And then the old lizard brain, which
is the amygdala, the limbic system,

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:42.780
which really is the fight, flight,
or freeze part of the brain.

00:37:42.780 --> 00:37:48.000
And if you put me in MRI machine
and gave me a bunch of math problems

00:37:48.000 --> 00:37:52.080
and I was solving really well, and then
you showed me a picture of a big lion,

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:54.360
[INAUDIBLE] my reaction
time would go down.

00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:56.850
And so the way I think
about it is, when one is on,

00:37:56.850 --> 00:38:00.880
the other one tends to be off,
or like offline a little bit.

00:38:00.880 --> 00:38:03.780
And so you always want
to sort of be playing

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:06.960
with your brain to be able to
do what Mike was talking about.

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:08.865
We need to be training our brain.

00:38:08.865 --> 00:38:10.740
That's why I like talking
about brain health,

00:38:10.740 --> 00:38:13.500
instead of mental health, because
the brain's just another organ,

00:38:13.500 --> 00:38:16.050
and it can be actually
trained to work for us.

00:38:16.050 --> 00:38:17.790
Does that help, Anna?

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:18.850
ANNA BARTUSKA: That does.

00:38:18.850 --> 00:38:19.892
Thank you for those tips.

00:38:19.892 --> 00:38:22.860
I'm curious going back to
this kind of brain training

00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:26.730
and pivoting a little bit to Michael's
kind of analogy, too, of painting

00:38:26.730 --> 00:38:33.000
this picture, how do we actually start
to change these patterns, Michael?

00:38:33.000 --> 00:38:36.810
Like, how would you, how do we
foster this masterpiece of self

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:39.540
then in your work?

00:38:39.540 --> 00:38:40.890
MICHAEL PUETT: Absolutely.

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:43.920
I would, and this is in full
agreement with Luana's way

00:38:43.920 --> 00:38:46.680
of putting this, which
is beautiful, I would

00:38:46.680 --> 00:38:50.670
say you begin with the seemingly
most ludicrously mundane

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:52.380
aspects of your daily life.

00:38:52.380 --> 00:38:54.510
Because that's where
these patterns set in.

00:38:54.510 --> 00:38:56.620
And that's where we need to change them.

00:38:56.620 --> 00:39:01.050
So when I say ludicrously
mundane, I mean literally down

00:39:01.050 --> 00:39:06.060
to when you were walking down a
street, notice how little of what

00:39:06.060 --> 00:39:08.490
is around you you're
actually even seeing.

00:39:08.490 --> 00:39:14.550
When you're interacting with people,
notice how little of real interaction

00:39:14.550 --> 00:39:18.870
is actually occurring, including with
very close friends and family members.

00:39:18.870 --> 00:39:23.910
Note the degree to which your just
daily interactions or just, again,

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:25.050
these rote patterns.

00:39:25.050 --> 00:39:29.190
Note the degree to which your
emotions are these rote patterns.

00:39:29.190 --> 00:39:34.140
As we sadly know, family patterns can
set in and can continue for decades,

00:39:34.140 --> 00:39:35.940
and even move on to the next generation.

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:39.180
I mean, it's chilling the
degree to which we do this.

00:39:39.180 --> 00:39:43.198
Now, that's the negative part, so
immediately jumping to the good part,

00:39:43.198 --> 00:39:45.240
getting back to the heart
of your question, Anna.

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:47.560
So, how do we break this?

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:49.410
You go back to that mundane level.

00:39:49.410 --> 00:39:52.680
When you're walking down
a street on a daily basis,

00:39:52.680 --> 00:39:56.130
start training yourself to really
see what's going on around you.

00:39:56.130 --> 00:39:57.300
And this is training.

00:39:57.300 --> 00:39:59.490
You will not normally be doing this.

00:39:59.490 --> 00:40:02.460
If you try to think back,
what is your office space

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:03.900
like where you work every day?

00:40:03.900 --> 00:40:05.760
You probably can say
very little about it.

00:40:05.760 --> 00:40:09.730
What is the street like that you
walk to the bus every day on?

00:40:09.730 --> 00:40:13.290
You probably don't know, even
though you walk on it every day.

00:40:13.290 --> 00:40:16.230
You start training yourself
to start seeing things.

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.960
When you're interacting
with people, you start

00:40:18.960 --> 00:40:22.320
training yourself to start
really relating to them,

00:40:22.320 --> 00:40:24.570
as opposed to relating by rote pattern.

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:29.760
You begin to see, oh, this person who's
just mean, and that person is nice,

00:40:29.760 --> 00:40:35.610
you begin to realize you're in a pattern
where in the latter case, it's nice,

00:40:35.610 --> 00:40:38.220
that's good, but this
person who's mean, you're

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:43.030
probably doing something that's pulling
out that negative energy from them.

00:40:43.030 --> 00:40:46.770
So you're training yourself to
begin to really relate to them.

00:40:46.770 --> 00:40:49.860
You're beginning to sense, what
am I doing that's drawing out

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:52.560
that person's anger or resentment.

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:55.740
How do I shift this with
this family member who I just

00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:58.530
keep having this daily fight with?

00:40:58.530 --> 00:41:05.550
How can I shift that a little bit by
simply playing with different emotions,

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:09.850
taking on different roles with that
person, shifting how I interact

00:41:09.850 --> 00:41:10.350
with them?

00:41:10.350 --> 00:41:14.160
Oftentimes, at the most ludicrously
basic level at the beginning,

00:41:14.160 --> 00:41:18.900
just using different tones of voice,
using different body language.

00:41:18.900 --> 00:41:23.560
These seemingly mundane things,
once you begin working with them,

00:41:23.560 --> 00:41:27.240
you begin realizing you can really
shift the degree to which you're

00:41:27.240 --> 00:41:31.110
relating to the world around
you, to people around you.

00:41:31.110 --> 00:41:34.290
And once you begin to
sense that shift, then you

00:41:34.290 --> 00:41:37.170
can begin doing it at
an even higher level.

00:41:37.170 --> 00:41:40.050
You can begin really working
on your relationships,

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:45.390
really getting a sense of why
with my coworker, just involved

00:41:45.390 --> 00:41:50.040
in this constant jealousy
resentment competitive battle.

00:41:50.040 --> 00:41:54.930
And it's amazing how these
seemingly unchangeable relationships

00:41:54.930 --> 00:41:58.920
can be altered dramatically
within literally a few weeks.

00:41:58.920 --> 00:41:59.940
It's incredible.

00:41:59.940 --> 00:42:04.980
And families maybe takes a few months,
but still, it is absolutely doable.

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:09.270
But again, you begin with the most
mundane aspects of your daily life.

00:42:09.270 --> 00:42:13.220
And then you begin working from there.

00:42:13.220 --> 00:42:14.962
LUANA MARQUES: I love this.

00:42:14.962 --> 00:42:17.680
ANNA BARTUSKA: I do, too.

00:42:17.680 --> 00:42:20.540
LUANA MARQUES: The reason I love
this, Mike, for many, many years,

00:42:20.540 --> 00:42:22.760
whenever I started to
work with a new client,

00:42:22.760 --> 00:42:25.290
they wanted, everybody
wants to advance fast.

00:42:25.290 --> 00:42:27.300
We all do, that's the human condition.

00:42:27.300 --> 00:42:31.020
And I'd say to them, how often do you
even notice your shower in the morning.

00:42:31.020 --> 00:42:33.860
Because most of us are having
an argument with somebody

00:42:33.860 --> 00:42:35.930
later in the day on that shower.

00:42:35.930 --> 00:42:37.910
Or like brushing your teeth.

00:42:37.910 --> 00:42:40.820
How many people actually pay
attention to brushing their teeth?

00:42:40.820 --> 00:42:44.870
Most of the time, you're already
on to-do list, six things ahead,

00:42:44.870 --> 00:42:46.530
trying to get something else done.

00:42:46.530 --> 00:42:49.370
And so actually think there
is magic on the mundane,

00:42:49.370 --> 00:42:52.550
because those are the moments
that we wake up to oh,

00:42:52.550 --> 00:42:57.000
my God, my life is
automatic pilot so much.

00:42:57.000 --> 00:42:59.700
And if we can't even
notice those moments,

00:42:59.700 --> 00:43:02.310
then it's really hard to
change the more complex thing.

00:43:02.310 --> 00:43:05.820
So I love your answer.

00:43:05.820 --> 00:43:08.310
ANNA BARTUSKA: Yeah, I really
appreciate both your answers.

00:43:08.310 --> 00:43:11.490
To play just a little bit of
devil's advocate and the thought

00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:13.950
that I had coming to mind,
my own chatter pattern

00:43:13.950 --> 00:43:20.560
here, is, are we missing
anything by being in the present?

00:43:20.560 --> 00:43:24.520
And I mean that, and by starting
to notice some of these patterns

00:43:24.520 --> 00:43:28.570
more, like the conversation you
mentioned, Luana, about in the future,

00:43:28.570 --> 00:43:33.250
talking with trying to plan
something, if we're always

00:43:33.250 --> 00:43:37.710
noticing what is around us, do we lose
anything by not being able to plan?

00:43:37.710 --> 00:43:40.325
Help me break that down further.

00:43:40.325 --> 00:43:42.450
LUANA MARQUES: I don't
think we're losing anything.

00:43:42.450 --> 00:43:44.790
I think we are thriving.

00:43:44.790 --> 00:43:48.090
Because we are not, I don't think either
Mike and I are saying one of the costs

00:43:48.090 --> 00:43:50.940
that, of course, we have to
have moments of planning.

00:43:50.940 --> 00:43:55.140
But let me just argue that the shower
perhaps, is a great place to think.

00:43:55.140 --> 00:43:57.600
But also, sometimes we're
just replaying things.

00:43:57.600 --> 00:44:00.270
We're not actually planning.

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:05.130
We're really in that anxious brain just
ruminating or thinking about thinking

00:44:05.130 --> 00:44:09.070
or just worrying about something,
versus what would look different

00:44:09.070 --> 00:44:11.565
would be like, OK, I have
a presentation later today

00:44:11.565 --> 00:44:13.440
and I'm just running
through my presentation.

00:44:13.440 --> 00:44:15.420
That's planning, that's practicing.

00:44:15.420 --> 00:44:18.630
But that's not-- the argument
I think we're both making,

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:23.580
is this idea that automatic pilot robs
you the ability to just be present.

00:44:23.580 --> 00:44:28.470
And actually, it takes a lot more
energy to worry than to be present.

00:44:28.470 --> 00:44:30.960
And once you're present,
your sleep is better,

00:44:30.960 --> 00:44:33.960
you engage in a way that's
much more meaningful.

00:44:33.960 --> 00:44:36.000
And so I don't I don't think it robs us.

00:44:36.000 --> 00:44:37.530
Michael, what do you think?

00:44:37.530 --> 00:44:39.780
MICHAEL PUETT: I fully agree.

00:44:39.780 --> 00:44:44.310
So often when we think of plans,
we think of them in the sense

00:44:44.310 --> 00:44:47.370
that well, it's the way
to get ahead in life

00:44:47.370 --> 00:44:51.750
is that if I decide well in
advance I want to do X, Y, and Z,

00:44:51.750 --> 00:44:55.990
so well in advance can mean what I
want to become 20 years from now.

00:44:55.990 --> 00:45:00.360
But it can also mean, what I'll do in
that meeting that I have later today.

00:45:00.360 --> 00:45:03.540
Either way, we're focused on that plan.

00:45:03.540 --> 00:45:07.710
And then everything is aimed at
trying to get us to that point.

00:45:07.710 --> 00:45:10.890
The danger is exactly
as you said, Luana.

00:45:10.890 --> 00:45:14.820
That means we are literally
not seeing anything going on

00:45:14.820 --> 00:45:21.060
around us, because we're so focused on
being that X. At the meeting or that

00:45:21.060 --> 00:45:22.590
X in 20 years.

00:45:22.590 --> 00:45:26.730
And everything is aimed at
that, which means precisely

00:45:26.730 --> 00:45:30.660
because we're not seeing what's
going on around us, we literally

00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:33.130
cease to interact with those around us.

00:45:33.130 --> 00:45:35.250
We cease to see what is around us.

00:45:35.250 --> 00:45:38.890
And it means we cease to
be open to possibilities.

00:45:38.890 --> 00:45:41.670
So going to the extreme
example, that in 20 years

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:47.250
I will be X, well, if you're really
open to the world around you,

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:49.200
there are probably,
I'll take out probably.

00:45:49.200 --> 00:45:53.160
There are possibilities that will
be opening up to you constantly

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:56.040
that you will literally never ever see.

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:59.490
And precisely by being
open to them, that

00:45:59.490 --> 00:46:01.650
is what will allow you to really grow.

00:46:01.650 --> 00:46:03.240
Now, are you planning?

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:03.990
Yes.

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:07.110
But in a very different way
than we usually use that term.

00:46:07.110 --> 00:46:12.310
Then you are really growing as a human
being in this incredibly creative mode

00:46:12.310 --> 00:46:13.570
that we've been mentioning.

00:46:13.570 --> 00:46:19.200
And that is how you can really be
something extraordinary in 20 years,

00:46:19.200 --> 00:46:21.840
or even at that meeting
later in the day.

00:46:21.840 --> 00:46:25.600
Because you're living your life
and you'll be more fully present.

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:27.270
So I agree completely.

00:46:27.270 --> 00:46:30.240
It sounds like a paradox
to say by being more

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:33.600
present you can be more
fully open to the future,

00:46:33.600 --> 00:46:37.350
but I think it is absolutely the case.

00:46:37.350 --> 00:46:39.580
ANNA BARTUSKA: That's
radical, and so helpful.

00:46:39.580 --> 00:46:41.440
We have a few more questions coming in.

00:46:41.440 --> 00:46:45.610
I want to try to get to some
of them before our time is up.

00:46:45.610 --> 00:46:48.240
And I think one of
these links a little bit

00:46:48.240 --> 00:46:51.570
to what we've been talking about in
terms of potentially being present,

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:54.810
and also kind of when our brain
starts to run about different things.

00:46:54.810 --> 00:46:59.610
The question is, "What can we do
to care less about other people's

00:46:59.610 --> 00:47:03.400
negative opinions?"

00:47:03.400 --> 00:47:05.310
I'm seeing smiles from both of you.

00:47:05.310 --> 00:47:07.370
Who wants to jump in?

00:47:07.370 --> 00:47:09.107
MICHAEL PUETT: Luana, please.

00:47:09.107 --> 00:47:09.940
LUANA MARQUES: Sure.

00:47:09.940 --> 00:47:19.170
I mean, there's two types of, I'm going
to b e very sort of black and white.

00:47:19.170 --> 00:47:22.020
But there's two ways we can
choose to live our lives.

00:47:22.020 --> 00:47:27.250
One is an emotion-driven life, and
the other one is a value-driven life.

00:47:27.250 --> 00:47:31.410
And whenever we care a lot
about what other people think,

00:47:31.410 --> 00:47:33.960
we're very likely in a
narrative about ourselves

00:47:33.960 --> 00:47:37.620
that's being run by our own emotions.

00:47:37.620 --> 00:47:41.740
And so it's really this idea
of shifting your perspective.

00:47:41.740 --> 00:47:44.940
So, in my book, Bold Move,
which is coming out next week,

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:48.150
I talk very openly about
the shifts I have to make.

00:47:48.150 --> 00:47:49.770
And what do I mean by shifting?

00:47:49.770 --> 00:47:52.680
It's really learning
to talk to yourself as

00:47:52.680 --> 00:47:55.050
if you're talking to your best friend.

00:47:55.050 --> 00:47:57.090
It's becoming a bit of a detective.

00:47:57.090 --> 00:47:59.490
It's questioning the
narrative in our brains.

00:47:59.490 --> 00:48:01.920
And so if we are really,
really caring, it

00:48:01.920 --> 00:48:04.470
means we're saying
something about ourselves.

00:48:04.470 --> 00:48:08.400
Very likely something like, I'm
not enough, I'm not good enough,

00:48:08.400 --> 00:48:09.360
I'm unlovable.

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:13.590
And that narrative is really
what's feeding your insecurities

00:48:13.590 --> 00:48:15.510
about what other people think of you.

00:48:15.510 --> 00:48:18.010
And that's why I said
a few minutes earlier,

00:48:18.010 --> 00:48:20.190
it's really important
to work on the self

00:48:20.190 --> 00:48:22.740
before you work on the
context around you.

00:48:22.740 --> 00:48:24.340
Because both need work.

00:48:24.340 --> 00:48:27.870
But if you're walking around
with lenses that are distorted,

00:48:27.870 --> 00:48:30.240
I think of them as like glasses.

00:48:30.240 --> 00:48:32.580
It's like we have magnifying glasses on.

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:35.940
We've worn them for so
long that we are not

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:38.530
even aware of what we're
saying to ourselves.

00:48:38.530 --> 00:48:43.560
And so, it's time to take the glasses
off, literally, and really examine

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:45.510
what the narrative in your brain is.

00:48:45.510 --> 00:48:48.300
Shift, talk to yourself
as if you're best friend.

00:48:48.300 --> 00:48:50.490
And last thing I'll say,
and I'll throw it to you,

00:48:50.490 --> 00:48:52.230
Michael, is thoughts are not facts.

00:48:52.230 --> 00:48:53.160
Let me say that again.

00:48:53.160 --> 00:48:54.118
Thoughts are not facts.

00:48:54.118 --> 00:48:56.693
I know we want them to be
facts, but they're not.

00:48:56.693 --> 00:48:59.110
They are just something that
we're coming up in our brain.

00:48:59.110 --> 00:49:03.600
And if it's arbitrary anyhow, why do
we have to be so mean to ourselves?

00:49:06.945 --> 00:49:08.445
ANNA BARTUSKA: Such a good question.

00:49:11.590 --> 00:49:13.770
I really appreciate that response.

00:49:13.770 --> 00:49:15.550
I think it's really, really helpful.

00:49:15.550 --> 00:49:18.930
And I appreciate both of your tips that
you're kind of giving throughout this.

00:49:18.930 --> 00:49:20.763
I think there's so much
richness, and I hope

00:49:20.763 --> 00:49:24.990
everyone is taking notes as much
as I am, too, while doing this.

00:49:24.990 --> 00:49:30.990
So we've had a few questions come up,
and I'd love to hear from both of you

00:49:30.990 --> 00:49:35.810
about your views on meditation.

00:49:35.810 --> 00:49:38.060
Specifically, the
questions are around, do

00:49:38.060 --> 00:49:42.320
you feel it can help reduce
anxiety if practiced regularly.

00:49:42.320 --> 00:49:47.180
Do you think that we can improve mental
health and well-being by meditation.

00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:48.980
And just your general views on it.

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:50.457
Michael, let's start with you.

00:49:50.457 --> 00:49:51.290
MICHAEL PUETT: Sure.

00:49:51.290 --> 00:49:54.860
I'll answer with an
emphatic yes, absolutely.

00:49:54.860 --> 00:49:59.960
But let me also mention a danger of
the ways that we will often do it.

00:49:59.960 --> 00:50:03.890
So, a danger of the ways
we often do meditation

00:50:03.890 --> 00:50:09.920
is simply to say, OK, to deal with all
of my stress and unhappiness and anger

00:50:09.920 --> 00:50:13.110
and resentment and blah blah,
blah, blah, blah, our normal lives,

00:50:13.110 --> 00:50:17.210
the way we deal with that is
stopping for a few moments,

00:50:17.210 --> 00:50:19.850
breathing deeply, calming down.

00:50:19.850 --> 00:50:24.050
And then that will give me the strength
to return to my life unchanged.

00:50:24.050 --> 00:50:27.120
And needless to say,
that's not the goal.

00:50:27.120 --> 00:50:31.610
The goal is not to calm yourself down so
you can live your life, your miserable

00:50:31.610 --> 00:50:33.980
life, unchanged.

00:50:33.980 --> 00:50:36.630
The goal is to change how
you're living your life.

00:50:36.630 --> 00:50:42.170
And so meditation works if you do
the work of what that really means,

00:50:42.170 --> 00:50:47.060
which is, it has to affect what happens
when you walk out of that meditation

00:50:47.060 --> 00:50:50.360
room or off of that mat
where you do your meditation

00:50:50.360 --> 00:50:52.610
or in the corner where
you're breathing deeply.

00:50:52.610 --> 00:50:56.390
It is not helping if it doesn't
really affect how you live your life.

00:50:56.390 --> 00:50:58.140
And that's the key.

00:50:58.140 --> 00:51:02.780
So, meditation can help,
but only if then when

00:51:02.780 --> 00:51:05.870
you are dealing with
your daily life, you're

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:12.260
really using that momentary calm you
felt to really alter your patterns,

00:51:12.260 --> 00:51:14.150
really work with the world.

00:51:14.150 --> 00:51:18.680
If you're simply calming yourself
down to continue with life unchanged,

00:51:18.680 --> 00:51:20.840
then it can be downright dangerous.

00:51:20.840 --> 00:51:25.100
You're just giving yourself a good drug
to be able to make it through your life

00:51:25.100 --> 00:51:26.490
without changing anything.

00:51:26.490 --> 00:51:30.650
So the key is, you must do, going
back to Luana's key point here,

00:51:30.650 --> 00:51:34.700
you must do that work on a daily
basis to really alter your life.

00:51:34.700 --> 00:51:38.150
And meditation is helpful
if it's part of that program

00:51:38.150 --> 00:51:40.700
of allowing you to do so.

00:51:40.700 --> 00:51:41.990
LUANA MARQUES: I love this.

00:51:41.990 --> 00:51:46.200
I can't tell you how many people I've
worked with that avoid the meditation.

00:51:46.200 --> 00:51:49.010
They're like, if I just meditate,
then it's going to be fine.

00:51:49.010 --> 00:51:50.840
You're just avoiding discomfort.

00:51:50.840 --> 00:51:53.450
And there's no life without discomfort.

00:51:53.450 --> 00:51:57.290
And so there's so much science showing
that meditation can change our brain.

00:51:57.290 --> 00:52:00.440
But the work is on
and off that question.

00:52:00.440 --> 00:52:04.820
And off is perhaps even more
important than on the question.

00:52:04.820 --> 00:52:06.050
I love your point, Michael.

00:52:06.050 --> 00:52:09.220
That's so true.

00:52:09.220 --> 00:52:10.880
ANNA BARTUSKA: Really helpful.

00:52:10.880 --> 00:52:12.080
Thank you so much.

00:52:12.080 --> 00:52:15.430
I have one more question that
I'd like to pose to both of you

00:52:15.430 --> 00:52:18.400
that I think is an excellent question
that we got from the audience

00:52:18.400 --> 00:52:19.870
to wrap us up today.

00:52:19.870 --> 00:52:23.830
You both have different courses talking
about happiness and living a good life,

00:52:23.830 --> 00:52:26.930
talking about managing anxiety
and living a bold life.

00:52:26.930 --> 00:52:29.740
And so the final question
for today is, can we

00:52:29.740 --> 00:52:35.440
be happy and live with
anxiety at the same time?

00:52:35.440 --> 00:52:37.420
Michael, I'll start with you.

00:52:37.420 --> 00:52:39.280
MICHAEL PUETT: Wonderful question.

00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:43.060
And the answer is, absolutely.

00:52:43.060 --> 00:52:46.420
And let me even introduce
another term here

00:52:46.420 --> 00:52:49.720
coming from the Chinese
tradition, which is joy.

00:52:49.720 --> 00:52:55.060
And what they mean by joy is,
often when we think of happiness,

00:52:55.060 --> 00:52:58.690
you can have a deep happiness
that is joyous in the way

00:52:58.690 --> 00:53:00.370
that they will define it--

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:05.110
--but often when we think of
things like happiness and anxiety

00:53:05.110 --> 00:53:13.420
and anger and resentment, all of our
usual emotional responses to the world,

00:53:13.420 --> 00:53:16.060
the sense is, we try
to think, OK, I want

00:53:16.060 --> 00:53:18.730
more of the moments when I feel
happy and fewer of the moments

00:53:18.730 --> 00:53:20.650
when I'm feeling anxious.

00:53:20.650 --> 00:53:25.570
And the view that will come in from this
notion of joy is, what you're trying

00:53:25.570 --> 00:53:27.970
to get is this deep feeling of joy.

00:53:27.970 --> 00:53:30.220
It's this deep connection.

00:53:30.220 --> 00:53:32.560
Joy here, the Chinese
term is the same as music.

00:53:32.560 --> 00:53:36.190
I think it's like resonating
musically with those around you,

00:53:36.190 --> 00:53:37.940
with the world around you.

00:53:37.940 --> 00:53:39.850
That's what you're trying to get.

00:53:39.850 --> 00:53:42.580
Now, getting back to the heart of
the question, that doesn't mean

00:53:42.580 --> 00:53:43.820
horrible things won't happen to you.

00:53:43.820 --> 00:53:44.860
Of course, it's life.

00:53:44.860 --> 00:53:46.318
Horrible things will happen to you.

00:53:46.318 --> 00:53:48.980
You'll be sad, you'll be
anxious, that will happen.

00:53:48.980 --> 00:53:50.980
But what you're training
yourself to reach,

00:53:50.980 --> 00:53:54.272
is a point where that
anxiety isn't debilitating.

00:53:54.272 --> 00:53:56.980
And again, going back to the heart
of what we've been mentioning,

00:53:56.980 --> 00:54:00.760
that anxiety isn't a product
of your patterns in life.

00:54:00.760 --> 00:54:04.000
It's simply a momentary thing you feel.

00:54:04.000 --> 00:54:06.880
You sense the pattern playing
out, you work against it.

00:54:06.880 --> 00:54:08.380
Something horrible happens.

00:54:08.380 --> 00:54:11.650
Of course, it's sad, but
then you work around that,

00:54:11.650 --> 00:54:15.550
because you have this depth of joy
that you've been training yourself

00:54:15.550 --> 00:54:18.850
for years to slowly develop.

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:21.470
And therefore, getting back
to the heart of the question,

00:54:21.470 --> 00:54:23.710
the answer is emphatically yes.

00:54:23.710 --> 00:54:25.630
But not, again, in the
happiness and the sense

00:54:25.630 --> 00:54:29.600
that we often use it, like
something good happened, I'm happy.

00:54:29.600 --> 00:54:33.790
I found an ice cream cone in
my fridge that tastes good.

00:54:33.790 --> 00:54:35.410
I feel momentarily happy.

00:54:35.410 --> 00:54:37.690
Sure, that can exist with
anxiety, but you're just,

00:54:37.690 --> 00:54:39.430
emotions are going back and forth.

00:54:39.430 --> 00:54:41.440
What you're seeking is
this joyousness that

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:45.880
allows you to sense that great
happiness, work with the anxiety

00:54:45.880 --> 00:54:49.030
and work with all of the other
emotions, because it comes out

00:54:49.030 --> 00:54:52.410
of this depth of joy.

00:54:52.410 --> 00:54:53.730
LUANA MARQUES: So agree.

00:54:53.730 --> 00:54:56.370
You know, my grandmother
used to describe this

00:54:56.370 --> 00:55:00.480
as when people are changing,
they tend to be like a rock.

00:55:00.480 --> 00:55:02.670
They want to stay where they are.

00:55:02.670 --> 00:55:05.940
And to Michael's point, it's
like this black and white.

00:55:05.940 --> 00:55:08.190
I mean, they're happy or sad.

00:55:08.190 --> 00:55:11.370
And my grandmother used to
say that to have a happy life,

00:55:11.370 --> 00:55:13.050
you have to be more like the water.

00:55:13.050 --> 00:55:16.650
That you go around the rock, you go on
top, you go on bottom, you're flexible.

00:55:16.650 --> 00:55:19.350
And in my brain, that's
cognitive flexibility.

00:55:19.350 --> 00:55:23.340
It's that ability of handling
the challenging moments of life

00:55:23.340 --> 00:55:26.280
with more grace, with
more skills, actually.

00:55:26.280 --> 00:55:29.240
And knowing that there are
going to be ups and downs,

00:55:29.240 --> 00:55:32.460
but if we practice the skills that
Mike and I have been talking about,

00:55:32.460 --> 00:55:35.310
you are much better
prepared to handle the lows.

00:55:35.310 --> 00:55:38.760
And you can really be present
in those moments of joy.

00:55:38.760 --> 00:55:41.190
And I love the idea of joy
even more, because it's

00:55:41.190 --> 00:55:44.970
those little micro moments
when you look at somebody,

00:55:44.970 --> 00:55:49.320
you have been working all day, and I
try to practice this the end of my day,

00:55:49.320 --> 00:55:52.530
when I go see my kid, I
like to really look at him.

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:55.200
Can I have a little moment
of just being present?

00:55:55.200 --> 00:55:58.150
Because often, we just
going on to the next thing.

00:55:58.150 --> 00:56:02.610
And so I think we can live
incredible life with anxiety,

00:56:02.610 --> 00:56:06.420
as long as we are moving towards
the things that matter the most.

00:56:06.420 --> 00:56:09.900
As long as we are value-driven
and we are present.

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:13.590
And honestly, you make
the anxiety work for you.

00:56:13.590 --> 00:56:16.500
At least, I do.

